# NorthNarrow — process comm allowlist (default, v1)
#
# Tappa 10.5 (D1). PROGRAM NAMES that are EXEMPT from the Tappa 10.5
# process detection rules (R011..R017) and from the io_uring and BPF
# rules (NN-L-IOU-*, NN-L-BPF-*). A site that runs a normally-suspicious
# tool from trusted automation adds its name here to silence the rule
# WITHOUT a code change.
#
# An entry is matched on what the kernel executed (external audit
# NETR-003 class): the executable's basename, or the script's an
# interpreter runs (`.py` dropped), which must lie in a root-owned
# system tree (/usr, /opt, /bin, /sbin, /lib, /snap, /nix/store). A
# 15-character entry also covers the longer names the kernel truncates
# a process name to (`unattended-upgr` for `unattended-upgrade`). A
# process that only CALLS itself like an entry (prctl(PR_SET_NAME), a
# copied binary) is not exempt; when its executable cannot be read
# (it exited first) the verdict is recorded instead of acted on.
#
# Format: one name per line; `#` introduces a comment; blank lines
# ignored; NO `+`/`-` prefixes (those are .local-only). Operators
# tune via the overlay at /etc/northnarrow/process-comm-allowlist.local
# with `+name` (add) / `-name` (re-enable detection on a default).
# Every disabled default emits a boot WARN.
#
# IMPORTANT — what NOT to put here: this file holds TRUSTED ACTORS,
# not the detection targets. The process rules already key on
# specific tool names (e.g. insmod / modprobe / setcap / nsenter);
# adding those here would disable the rule globally. The conservative
# default below is limited to package-manager + configuration-
# management actors that legitimately perform privileged operations
# on a fresh-install Linux host and are the most common false-positive
# sources. Extend per-site via the .local overlay.

# Package managers
apt
apt-get
dpkg
dnf
yum
rpm
snapd
unattended-upgr

# Configuration management / provisioning
cloud-init
ansible
puppet
salt-minion
chef-client
